 Axios AM Deep Dive 
 
 
 
 
 By Mike Allen &middot; Oct 31, 2026 
 
 
 🦾 Welcome to the first major election of the AI era. In this special midterm edition, we're exploring how AI and data centers are reshaping campaigns, dividing communities, and forcing voters and local leaders to confront what the AI boom means for their futures.

 This newsletter was reported and written by Axios' tech, business and energy experts ... orchestrated by director of special projects Natalie Daher ... and copy edited by Amy Stern. Smart Brevity™ count: 1,498 words ... 5½ mins. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: AI boom meets the ballot box 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: Brandon Bell/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Technology has become a defining issue in the 2026 midterms — and AI data centers are the punching bag.

 🥊 Why it matters: It's not just about noise pollution and curb appeal. Data centers have become a lightning rod for growing resentment over who gets rich from the AI boom and who bears the cost, Axios' Maria Curi and Erica Pandey report. 💬 Tatishe Nteta , a political scientist at UMass Amherst, says: "It's a reflection of what's increasingly becoming an easy target — tech giants and AI."

 He calls the backlash "a turn against the billionaire class from both the left and the right." 🗳️ Driving the news: Opposition to data centers reached a fever pitch in the months leading up to the midterms. Once an obscure infrastructure issue, data centers have become a political liability for candidates across the country.

 Voters in 10 states will decide on 41 data center ballot measures on Election Day, ranging from new requirements for developers to outright bans on construction. All six data center measures already decided this year — in New Hampshire, Wisconsin, Michigan and California — have gone against development, according to Ballotpedia . Data: Ballotpedia . Map: Erin Davis/Axios 🔎 Zoom in: Data center backlash is reshaping races and forcing politicians to change course. 

 In Texas, incumbent Gov. Greg Abbott (R) reversed course after years of courting the industry — halting new approvals pending an audit of projects — after a backlash in rural communities. Democratic challenger Gina Hinojosa supports a moratorium on new data centers until tougher rules are established. In Utah, an unlikely coalition of state leaders, local residents and environmental groups is opposing a data center project backed by Shark Tank's "Mr. Wonderful," Kevin O'Leary. In Ohio, incumbent Sen. Jon Husted (R) is on the defensive over past support for data centers as he fights to keep his seat in a race that could help decide Senate control. The state has 21 data center-related measures on Tuesday's ballot. The backlash is minting a new wave of candidates. Across the country, local activists galvanized by data center fights in their communities are running for office .

 ↔️ Between the lines: The opposition to data centers cuts across generations and party lines, though some groups are far more skeptical than others, per exclusive polling from the new Axios-CivicScience AI Sentiment Index .

 Overall, 43% of Americans oppose construction of data centers in their area, compared with 19% who support them. Another 38% are neutral. The generational divide is stark: Nearly two-thirds of baby boomers and older Americans (65%) oppose data centers, compared with 26% of Gen Z. A majority of independents (54%) and nearly half of Democrats (48%) oppose data centers. Republicans are almost evenly split, with 30% opposed and 29% supportive. 🇺🇸 The Trump factor: President Trump has complicated Republicans' efforts to navigate local backlash over data centers, declaring "we want them" at an Ohio rally and forcing the Husted campaign to distance itself from his remarks.

 Trump has called data centers a " Golden Goose " for the U.S. economy and praised companies like Meta for investing in local communities. Democrats are capitalizing on voter frustration and making data centers a major campaign issue . Share this story . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 2. 🔌 Who pays for the grid&#x27;s growing tab 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: Lexi Critchett/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Americans' electricity bills could be affected for years to come by the decisions being made now about who pays for the AI data center boom, Axios national energy correspondent Amy Harder reports. 

 💰 Why it matters: Policymakers increasingly want tech companies — not residential customers — to foot more of the bill. How it works: Data centers pay for the electricity they use and generally cover the cost of connecting to the grid. But the cost of building new power plants and broader electric grid upgrades can be passed on to households.

 🧮 Who ultimately pays depends on where a data center is built, how its utility charges customers and whether new protections take effect.

 More than half of states have taken or considered steps to protect residential customers, including special electricity rates and requirements that data centers cover more infrastructure costs. 👀 What to watch: Congress is considering bipartisan legislation to require large data centers to pay more of those costs — even if projects are canceled. 

