 6. 🏗️ One town&#x27;s bargain: New Albany, Ohio 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 New Albany, Ohio, has welcomed roughly 40 data centers operated by Meta, Google, AWS, Microsoft, Vantage Data Centers and others over the last decade. 

 The Columbus suburb courted these companies in exchange for millions in tax revenue and infrastructure investments — even as residents complain about noise, energy use and environmental impacts, Axios Macro co-author Courtenay Brown reports. 🛝 How it works: New Albany offers property tax breaks while requiring developers to make payments that help fund infrastructure, parks and other public improvements.

 Those payments generated roughly $10 million in 2024, equivalent to nearly one-fifth of the city's general fund revenue that year. A single hyperscale data center generated as much revenue for the city as it would collect in local income taxes from a company with $178 million in annual payroll. Data center revenue has also helped local schools avoid asking voters for new property tax levies. ⚖️ Reality check: The financial payoff of welcoming a data center depends on the deals communities negotiate — and whether residents feel the benefits outweigh the cost.

